  "article.txt": {
    "what": "a 180-word article about tiny urban forests",
    "made": "written",
    "transcript": "Why cities are planting tiny forests\n\nAcross Europe and Asia, cities are turning empty lots the size of a tennis court into dense \"tiny forests\". The method, developed by Japanese botanist Akira Miyawaki, plants three to five native saplings per square metre in enriched soil. The trees compete for light and grow quickly; supporters say a tiny forest can become self-sustaining in about three years.\n\nAdvocates point to cooler streets, more insects and birds, and a place for schoolchildren to learn. Critics say the evidence is still thin: many projects are too young to measure, and dense planting can mean many saplings die. Urban ecologists agree on one thing: a tiny forest is not a substitute for protecting existing large trees, which store far more carbon."
